Curriculum Design and Conversational Frameworks for Spanish-to-Catalan Digital Language AcquisitionThe engineering of a digital application intended to facilitate the acquisition of the Catalan language for Spanish-speaking users requires a rigorous, multidimensional architectural approach. This process must be deeply grounded in established institutional linguistic frameworks, sophisticated pedagogical methodologies, and the specific sociolinguistic realities of the target demographic. The cognitive transition from Spanish to Catalan is uniquely characterized by high lexical similarity and morphosyntactic parallelism. While this proximity facilitates rapid initial comprehension, it simultaneously introduces profound challenges regarding negative interlinguistic transfer, phonological divergence, and the persistent phenomena of false cognates. To construct an optimal educational technology framework, the instructional design must meticulously align with the standardized parameters established by the Consorci per a la Normalització Lingüística (CPNL) and the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR).This comprehensive analysis delineates the structural curriculum levels necessary for such an application, explicates the mechanics of lexical acquisition with a hyper-specific focus on mitigating Spanish-Catalan interference, and conceptualizes a framework for modeling highly contextualized, setting-specific conversations. By integrating these elements, alongside institutional resources and methodologies, the resulting software architecture can move beyond superficial translation to foster genuine communicative competence, sociolinguistic appropriateness, and cultural integration for the adult learner.Institutional Frameworks and Demographic AlignmentThe foundation of any robust adult language learning application operating within the Catalan ecosystem is a scalable, progressive curriculum mapped to recognized institutional standards. The paramount authority for adult language education in this domain is the Consorci per a la Normalització Lingüística (CPNL), an organism created in 1989 through the joint volition of the Generalitat de Catalunya and numerous municipalities and county councils. The primary objective of the CPNL is to facilitate the knowledge, use, and dissemination of the Catalan language across all societal spheres. Consequently, the courses organized by the CPNL strictly follow the contents established by the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), ensuring that the resulting certifications are equivalent to the official knowledge certificates issued by the Directorate General of Language Policy.For a digital application targeting Spanish speakers, the curriculum must adopt these institutional milestones while adapting the pedagogical pacing to account for the learners' existing Romance language scaffolding. The demographic reality of formal Catalan education reveals a pronounced bifurcation that the software must anticipate. Statistical observations indicate that learners in the foundational levels (A1, A2, and B1) are overwhelmingly Spanish speakers. A significant portion of this cohort originates from other autonomous communities within Spain, having relocated to Catalan-speaking territories—such as Catalonia or the Balearic Islands (e.g., Ibiza)—primarily for employment and labor-related motivations. Conversely, in the advanced certification levels (B2, C1, and C2), the demographic shifts dramatically, with native Catalan speakers constituting the majority as they seek formal credentialing for administrative or professional advancement.This demographic distribution dictates a specific structural imperative for the application. The foundational modules must front-load functional, transactional, and integration-focused content designed for economic migrants and professional expatriates. The advanced modules must transition seamlessly into academic, normative, and highly formal registers, addressing the intricacies of the language at a near-native depth. Furthermore, prior to commencing the curriculum, the application must feature a robust diagnostic assessment system. The CPNL requires prospective students to determine their access level either by providing a recognized certificate of linguistic equivalence or by undergoing a specific placement test. Replicating this diagnostic gateway within the application ensures that users are immediately routed to the appropriate degree of difficulty, preventing both cognitive overload and user attrition due to insufficient challenge.Curricular Scaffolding: Adapting the CEFR for Spanish-to-Catalan AcquisitionThe application's core curriculum must be divided into the six primary levels dictated by the CEFR and adapted by the CPNL: Inicial (A1), Bàsic (A2), Elemental (B1), Intermedi (B2), Suficiència (C1), and Superior (C2). Each level introduces distinct communicative goals, morphosyntactic complexities, and pragmatic requirements.Curriculum LevelCEFR EquivalentPrimary Communicative Objective and Application Module FocusInstitutional Benchmarks & ResourcesInicialA1Interacting simply provided the interlocutor speaks slowly and clearly; targeting absolute beginners, sometimes without Latin alphabet knowledge. Application focus: phonological unlearning of Spanish constraints.Vincles (interactive didactic proposals for beginners).BàsicA2Comprehending phrases related to immediate importance (family, shopping, employment). Divided into three degrees (Bàsic 1, 2, 3). Application focus: daily transactional survival.Viure a les Illes Balears (image-word daily vocabulary).ElementalB1Expressing oneself with simple language adapted to the communicative situation, despite a lack of native fluency. Application focus: situational adaptability and text extraction.SpeakCat (survival courses in university contexts).IntermediB2Expressing thoughts on a wide range of topics precisely, fluently, and coherently. Application focus: formal social interactions and informative text production.Itineraris d'aprenentatge (autocorrective B2 exercises); Dictats en línia.SuficiènciaC1Producing complex, argumentative texts in private, social, and labor domains with absolute adequacy and cohesion. Application focus: professional documentation and debate.Argumenta (resource for producing formal oral and written texts).SuperiorC2Deepening knowledge in specific, nuanced aspects of the language; mastering high-level theoretical concepts alongside practical domain expertise. Application focus: metalinguistic awareness.CPNL Superior C2 Grammar Compendium; Advanced Dictats en línia.Table 1: Macro-level curriculum alignment integrating CPNL standards with digital application module focus.The Foundational Tier: Inicial (A1) and Bàsic (A2)The Inicial (A1) level is fundamentally designed for basic users who require the most elementary tools for interaction. The CPNL defines the objective of this level as enabling learners to interact simply, provided the interlocutor is willing to assist. While institutional courses note that this level often accommodates individuals entirely unfamiliar with the Latin alphabet, an application targeting Spanish speakers can bypass alphabetical instruction. Instead, the A1 module must hyper-focus on phonological discrepancies, introducing the Catalan vocalic system and its complex consonantal inventory. Resources such as Vincles, a material produced by the Generalitat de Catalunya that includes interactive didactic proposals to exercise linguistic skills for absolute beginners, serve as an excellent pedagogical model for these early stages.The Bàsic (A2) level represents the critical phase of functional independence in highly predictable environments. An A2 user must comprehend phrases and habitual expressions related to areas of immediate importance, such as personal basic information, family, local geography, and employment. The application must structure this level into progressive sub-modules (akin to the CPNL's Bàsic 1, Bàsic 2, and Bàsic 3 degrees). During Bàsic 1, the learner familiarizes themselves with the linguistic system, sounds, and spellings, beginning to write short texts. By Bàsic 2, the user must elaborate on brief texts regarding general interest topics and extract main ideas from short oral recordings. Upon completing the Bàsic tier, the user qualifies for the A2 certificate.The morphosyntactic syllabus for Bàsic 1 is extensive and serves as the grammatical engine for the learner. The CPNL dictates a rigorous progression of concepts that the software algorithm must systematically introduce, drill, and assess. The following structural table outlines the mandatory grammatical constructs for the Bàsic 1 module and their specific developmental focus for Spanish-speaking users.Grammatical CategorySpecific CPNL Curriculum Elements (Bàsic 1)Application Algorithmic Focus for Spanish SpeakersNominal ElementsThe article; nouns and adjectives; gender of nouns/adjectives; number of nouns/adjectives.Highlighting gender discrepancies (e.g., masculine Catalan nouns that are feminine in Spanish) and complex pluralization rules absent in Spanish (e.g., words ending in -sc, -ig).DeterminersDemonstratives; numerals; interrogatives; possessives; quantifiers and indefinites.Drilling the syntactic positioning and phonetic linking of possessives and demonstratives.Verbal MorphologyVerbs general; verbal tenses; the indicative (present, future, conditional); non-personal verb forms.Emphasizing the distinct regular conjugations (first, second, third) and the formation of the future/conditional stems which often diverge from Spanish.Irregular Verbs & PronominalsVerbs of the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd conjugation; specific focus on ser, estar, anar, anar-se'n, and venir; verbs with pronouns.Disambiguating the nuanced differences in the use of ser and estar regarding location and state, which do not map 1:1 with Spanish rules.Pronominal SystemsStrong personal pronouns (pronoms personals forts); weak pronouns (pronoms febles).The pronoms febles represent the highest cognitive barrier. The app requires dedicated spaced-repetition modules for apostrophization and clitic combinations.ModifiersAdverbs; prepositions.Addressing prepositional divergence, specifically the Catalan use of en versus a compared to the Spanish locative and temporal en.Table 2: Morphosyntactic progression for the Bàsic 1 curriculum based on CPNL guidelines.The Transitional and Advanced Tiers: Elemental (B1) to Superior (C2)At the Elemental (B1) level, the application shifts its instructional design from purely transactional exchanges to situational adaptability. The learner must express themselves with language that adapts appropriately to the communicative situation, even if it lacks the absolute fluency of a native speaker. Structured into Elemental 1, 2, and 3, the curriculum demands that users comprehend written formal texts of general and specific dissemination, extract both explicit and implicit information, and communicate with sufficient correction. For Spanish speakers, B1 is the plateau where positive interlinguistic transfer yields diminishing returns; synthetic differences, such as the prohibition of prepositional accusatives, must be thoroughly addressed.The Intermedi (B2) level introduces a high degree of linguistic flexibility, dividing into three degrees designed to achieve a strong mastery of general language. According to CPNL parameters, a B2 user can express themselves across a wide array of topics, employing different registers precisely, fluently, and with acceptable coherence. The application's B2 modules must feature informative and instructive texts—such as brochures, prospectuses, and instructional manuals—and simulate semi-formal interviews. Users must be trained to write argumentative texts across private, social, and labor domains with proper linguistic cohesion. Resources such as the Itineraris d'aprenentatge for intermediate levels provide excellent templates for autocorrective materials and differentiated learning pathways.The apex of the curriculum involves the Suficiència (C1) and Superior (C2) levels, representing profound linguistic mastery and metalinguistic awareness. The C1 level requires the production of highly complex texts with complete structural integrity. The C2 level, designed for individuals needing to delve into highly specific and theoretical aspects of the Catalan language, involves theoretical concepts at a much higher degree, alongside the review of practical linguistic dominion. The application's advanced modules should leverage methodologies akin to Argumenta, an institutional resource that aids advanced users in producing formal oral and written texts through sophisticated tools and information architectures.Lexical Acquisition, Thematic Clustering, and the "Falsos Amics" PhenomenonVocabulary acquisition between closely related Romance languages is frequently deceptive. The cognitive ease of recognizing cognates often masks the necessity of precise lexical encoding, leading to fossilized errors. An effective digital application must transcend static flashcards by employing contextualized lexical integration, organizing vocabulary thematically while aggressively identifying points of semantic divergence.Thematic Lexical Clusters for Foundational ModulesBased on the CPNL's Bàsic 1 syllabus, the application's introductory vocabulary modules must be clustered around immediate, high-utility domains that serve as the semantic backbone for early conversational simulations. The application must systematically introduce the following lexical clusters:Identity and Social Navigation: Personal data (dades personals), formal and informal introductions (presentacions), and demonyms/gentilics (gentilicis).Physicality and Appearance: Physical descriptions, vocabulary related to clothing, colors, and the anatomical parts of the body (les parts del cos).Spatial and Temporal Orientation: The passage and organization of time, specific terminology for the hours and parts of the day, and vocabulary contrasting the city and the countryside (la ciutat i el camp).Interpersonal and Professional Domains: Terms regarding the family structure (la família), as well as trades and professions (oficis i professions).Abstract States and Courtesies: Forms of courtesy (formes de cortesia), and complex expressions detailing physical states, moods, offering advice, making suggestions, expressing opinions, and participating in debates.To facilitate the rapid acquisition of these clusters, the application should draw methodological inspiration from resources such as Viure a les Illes Balears, a compendium edited by the Directorate General of Language Policy that utilizes words accompanied by images referring to habitual communicative situations in daily life. Similarly, the Enxaneta resource offers an exhaustive collection of vocabularies, conjugated verbs, and exercises to perfect Catalan knowledge flexibly. Associating imagery and dynamic contexts directly with Catalan lexicons—bypassing the Spanish translation step—fosters a more direct acquisition pathway.The Asymmetry of False CognatesThe most insidious obstacle for a Spanish speaker learning Catalan is the phenomenon of falsos amics (false friends)—lexical items that exhibit orthographic or phonetic similarity across the two languages but possess divergent, sometimes contradictory, semantic values. A failure to map these accurately results in severe communicative breakdowns and frequently betrays a lack of sociolinguistic competence. The application must feature dedicated, algorithmic interventions to train users defensively against these discrepancies.An exhaustive lexicographical analysis reveals profound semantic shifts that the digital curriculum must explicitly target. The following structured data table categorizes critical false friends, juxtaposing the Spanish word, its actual (and sometimes surprising) Catalan meaning, the similar-sounding Catalan word, and the true Spanish equivalent of that similar-sounding term.Spanish TermMeaning if Used in Catalan ContextSimilar-Sounding Catalan TermTrue Spanish Equivalent of the Catalan TermAcostarse (to go to bed)N/A (Does not exist in this form. To go to bed is ajeure's or gitar-se)Acostar-se (to approach)AcercarseAfamado (famous)N/A (Famous is famós)Afamat (famished / hungry)HambrientoAlejar (to move away)N/A (To move away is allunyar)Alejar (to flutter / flap wings)AletearAlquilar (to rent)To introduce an alkyl group into an organic moleculeLlogar (to rent)Alquilar (rentar)Altillo (loft/attic)N/A (Attic is entresolat, sostremort, or mansarda)Altell (hill/knoll)Colina / Otero / AltozanoAmanecer (to dawn)N/A (Dawn is sortir el sol)Amanir (to dress a salad)AliñarAmpolla (blister)N/A (Blister is butllofa or bòfega)Ampolla (bottle)BotellaAndar (to walk)N/A (To walk is caminar)Anar (to go)IrApurar (to hurry/worry)To verify truth, exhaust resources, embarrass, or be left without moneyApurar (same as previous) / DepurarDepurar / Apurar (in the sense of clearing accounts)Avería (breakdown/damage)N/A (Breakdown is avaria)Averia (poultry/livestock)Ganado / Animales domésticosBesó (he/she kissed)N/A (Kissed is besà)Bessó (twin)GemeloBizca (cross-eyed)N/A (Cross-eyed is guenya)Visca (Hooray!)¡Viva!Bordar (to embroider)N/A (To embroider is brodar)Bordar (to bark)LadrarBorrego (sheep)N/A (Sheep is ovella)Borrego (a specific type of pastry)Cierta clase de bolloGolfas (derogatory: prostitutes/dishonest women)N/A (The derogatory term translates to esquenadretes or prostitutes)Les golfes (the attic / loft)El desvánJudía (green bean / Jewish woman)N/A (Green bean is mongeta or fesol. Jewish woman is jueva)Juia (lapwing bird)AvefríaPalo (stick / chore)N/A (Stick is bastó or fusta. Chore is mandra or llauna)Pal (pole / post)PosteRes (head of cattle / livestock)N/A (Livestock is bèstia or animal boví)Res (nothing)NadaSalpicar (to splash)N/A (To splash is esquitxar or esguitar)Salpicar (to sprinkle / to scatter)Esparcir / EspolvorearTumbarse (to lie down)N/A (To lie down is jaure, jeure, or estirar-se)Tombar-se (to turn around)Girar(se)Vaga (lazy woman / vague)N/A (Lazy is gandula or mandrosa)Vaga (labor strike)HuelgaTable 3: Comprehensive lexicographical mapping of Spanish-Catalan False Friends demanding targeted algorithmic intervention.The pedagogical implications of these discrepancies are profound. For instance, if a user in a simulated real estate dialogue attempts to say they want to rent an apartment, the application must strictly penalize the use of alquilar (which in Catalan is restricted exclusively to organic chemistry, meaning to alkylate) and dynamically enforce the use of llogar. Furthermore, the sociolinguistic danger of terms like golfas/golfes cannot be overstated; confusing a house's attic (les golfes) with a highly derogatory Spanish insult requires targeted contextual simulations to ensure the learner completely severs the cognitive link between the two.Modeling Conversational Settings and RegistersLanguage is fundamentally a tool for social action, necessitating that a digital learning environment immerse the user in cohesive, context-dependent communicative events. Drawing from the sociolinguistic needs of the Spanish-speaking demographic, the application must categorize its conversational modules across diverse physical, social, and professional settings. The instructional design should leverage multimedia methodologies observed in institutional projects to simulate reality accurately.Transactional and Survival Contexts (Levels A1-A2)The most immediate need for an adult economic migrant or student is transactional efficacy in public spaces. The application should model these dialogues around the concept of "survival Catalan," mirroring the objectives of the SpeakCat project, a survival course designed for foreign students in university contexts featuring autocorrective exercises across differentiated itineraries. Another foundational template is the A l'abast communication guide, a resource offering basic texts in Catalan translated into numerous languages, including Spanish, English, Arabic, and German, providing a multilingual baseline for basic interaction.Conversational scenarios in this tier must focus on the commercial sphere and civic navigation. When simulating interactions in a market or shop, the algorithmic focus must evaluate the user's correct application of the partitive pronoun en (e.g., stating En vull dos for "I want two of them"), a construct lacking a modern Spanish equivalent. The scenarios must also integrate local geography, training users to ask for directions and interact with transit systems utilizing the precise prepositions of place and imperative verb forms mandated by the CPNL.Relational and Subjective Contexts (Levels A2-B1)As learners advance, conversations must transition from formulaic transactions to relationship building, involving spontaneity and the negotiation of meaning. The application should integrate modules focusing on phone conversations and expressions of subjectivity, exactly as prescribed by the CPNL syllabus.During simulated telephonic interactions (converses telefòniques), the user must manage connectivity issues and schedule meetings, requiring a flawless command of the Catalan system of telling time. The application must meticulously train the quarter system (e.g., són tres quarts de vuit), which operates on an entirely different cognitive model than the Spanish time-telling format. Furthermore, scenarios set in casual environments must train the user to express physical and emotional states, debate lightly, and offer advice (estat físic i d'ànim, aconsellar, suggerir, opinar i debatre). A dialogue set in a pharmacy would perfectly test a user's ability to describe symptoms, ensuring they do not use the false friend ampolla to mean a blister (butllofa), but correctly identify it as a bottle of medicine.To build these conversational narratives, the application can model its user interface after the 4cats project, a didactic fiction series consisting of twenty episodes presenting daily situations. By accompanying video interactions with interactive transcripts, grammatical pop-ups, and glossaries, the software provides a holistic learning experience adapted for learners who have surpassed the initial 90 hours of study. Similarly, incorporating audiovisual methodologies utilized by initiatives like Easy Catalan—which creates online audiovisual material to support language learning—can vastly improve the authenticity of the conversational inputs.Academic and Professional Registers (Levels B1-C2)A significant segment of the target demographic requires integration into corporate, administrative, or academic environments, necessitating simulations of high-register, domain-specific conversations.For the university context, the application must integrate resources akin to Class-Talk, which provides 300 MP3 phrases to facilitate the comprehension of habitual phraseology in university settings. Additionally, referencing the University of Barcelona's collection of university conversation guides ensures the vocabulary reflects authentic academic discourse. Users acting as newly arrived transfer students must navigate registrar offices, interact formally with professors using the vostè pronoun, and coordinate peer presentations, seamlessly code-switching between formal and informal registers.For the professional workplace context, the application must construct branching dialogues representing job interviews and administrative tasks. The user must parse complex questions and construct answers demonstrating fluency and professional adequacy, fulfilling the CEFR B2/C1 requirements. This module should heavily integrate reading comprehension and document production tasks based on administrative drafting guides, similar to the CPNL's Documentació administrativa orientation models and the drafting guides utilized by various county councils. Furthermore, specialized occupational modules can be developed referencing materials like A la residència en català, a guide designed specifically for personnel attending to the elderly to increment their expression and comprehension capacities in the workplace.EdTech Implementation Strategies and Pedagogical ArchitectureTo actualize this curriculum within a digital environment, the application's architecture must translate institutional standards into engaging, adaptive pedagogical technologies.A critical component of this implementation involves multimodal input and autocorrective dictations to address orthographic challenges. The application must feature an online dictation system mirroring the institutional Dictats en línia resource, which targets the orthography of the intermediate (B2), proficiency (C1), and superior (C2) levels. Crucially, this system must include a recall feature (akin to the "Fem memòria" button) that reminds users of general spelling rules in the B levels, and presents complex exceptions in the C levels. This auditory feedback loop is essential for training Spanish speakers to perceive voiced versus unvoiced final consonants and the geminate L (l·l).Furthermore, the application must integrate a virtual learning environment accessible via user accounts, simulating the Espai alumnes utilized by the CPNL, providing asynchronous access to materials, grades, and community forums. To support self-directed learning, the software can curate links to external platforms offering Catalan audiobooks (Audiollibres), blogs providing materials from A1 to B2 such as the Blog CEPA Pitiüses, and comprehensive auto-learning guides like the Autoaprenentatge de català elaborated by the Linguistic Services of the University of Barcelona. By weaving these external cultural and linguistic touchpoints into the core digital curriculum, the application transforms from a static learning tool into a comprehensive portal for sociocultural integration, effectively bridging the linguistic divide for the Spanish-speaking demographic.